{"builder":"Build a new standalone tool, `jsonl-lint`, as the first workspace under `packages/`, and open one pull request for it on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test. The repo is currently empty of tools (`packages/` does not exist; the root `npm test` script prints `no workspaces under packages/` in that case). Follow CLAUDE.md: Node.js 22+, plain ES modules, no build step, zero runtime dependencies, tests with `node:test`.\n\nThe pull request has to meet every one of these acceptance lines (quoted from the issue):\n1. \"packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> prints <file>:<line>: <error> for every row that is not valid JSON (1-based lines, blank lines skipped, an unterminated last line checked) and exits 1 if any, 0 if none\"\n2. \"A missing file or no argument prints to stderr and exits 2\"\n3. \"packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js exports lint(text) returning [{ line, error }]\"\n4. \"The workspace has its own package.json (jsonl-lint, type module, test script node --test), no runtime dependencies, and node:test tests for a clean file, a bad middle row, a torn last row, blank lines and the exit codes\"\n5. \"packages/jsonl-lint/README.md exists and the root README lists the tool\"\n6. \"The change is one PR on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test whose body says Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/102, and npm test and the test workflow are green at its head\"\n\nSteps:\n1. Create `packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js`, an ES module with a named export `lint(text)`. Split the text on `\\n` and strip one trailing `\\r` from each row so CRLF files work. Skip rows that are empty (treating whitespace-only rows as blank too is recommended). Run `JSON.parse` on every other row, and collect `{ line, error }` for each row that fails, where `line` is the 1-based line number in the original text (blank lines still count toward numbering) and `error` is a non-empty string (the `JSON.parse` error message is fine). A final row with no trailing newline is still a row and must be checked, so a torn last line such as `{\"b\":` is reported. Return the array, which is `[]` for clean input. Put no I/O in this module.\n2. Create `packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js` with a `#!/usr/bin/env node` shebang. It takes one path argument. With no argument, print a usage message to stderr and exit 2. If the file cannot be read (missing, for example), print a message to stderr and exit 2. Otherwise read it as UTF-8, call `lint`, and for each result print `<file>:<line>: <error>` to stdout on its own line, where `<file>` is the path exactly as the user passed it. Exit 1 if there were any results and 0 if there were none, printing nothing to stdout on a clean file. Set `process.exitCode` instead of calling `process.exit()` right after writing, so stdout is flushed. Make the file executable (`chmod +x`).\n3. Create `packages/jsonl-lint/package.json` with `\"name\": \"jsonl-lint\"`, `\"type\": \"module\"`, `\"private\": true`, `\"bin\": { \"jsonl-lint\": \"bin/jsonl-lint.js\" }`, `\"engines\": { \"node\": \">=22\" }`, and `\"scripts\": { \"test\": \"node --test\" }`. Leave out `dependencies`, or leave it empty. With no path argument, `node --test` finds files such as `test/*.test.js`, so name the test files to match.\n4. Write `node:test` tests under `packages/jsonl-lint/test/` (for example `test/lint.test.js` and `test/cli.test.js`). Give each of these scenarios its own clearly named test: a clean file (lint returns `[]`, and the CLI exits 0 with empty stdout); a bad middle row (for example rows 1 and 3 valid, row 2 `{bad`, reported as line 2); a torn last row with no trailing newline (reported with its line number); blank lines (skipped, but line numbers after them stay correct, and an empty line is never reported); and the exit codes. For exit codes, spawn the CLI with `child_process.spawnSync(process.execPath, [binPath, file])` on fixtures written to `fs.mkdtempSync(os.tmpdir())` and assert 0 for clean, 1 for bad together with the exact `<file>:<line>: ` stdout prefix, 2 with non-empty stderr for a missing file, and 2 with non-empty stderr for no argument. Resolve paths from `import.meta.url` so the tests pass whether they run from the root or from the workspace.\n5. Write `packages/jsonl-lint/README.md`: what the tool does, usage (`node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file>`), the output format, the exit codes 0, 1 and 2, the `lint(text)` API, and how to run its tests.\n6. Update the root `README.md`. Its \"What is here\" section currently says there are no tools yet and `packages/` is empty, so replace that bullet with one that lists `packages/jsonl-lint` and says what it does in one line. Keep the rest accurate.\n7. Run `npm install` at the repository root so `package-lock.json` records the new workspace. CI runs `npm ci`, which fails if the lockfile is out of sync. Commit the updated `package-lock.json`.\n8. Check locally: `npm ci && npm test` at the root must pass and must show the jsonl-lint tests running, not `no workspaces under packages/`. Also run `npm test -w jsonl-lint`, and try the CLI by hand on a clean file, a bad file, a missing file and no argument, checking the output and the exit codes (`echo $?`).\n9. Commit, push, and open exactly one pull request on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test. Its body must contain the line `Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/102`. Wait for the `test` workflow (`.github/workflows/test.yml`) to finish on the PR head commit. If it fails, fix the cause, push again, and repeat until it is green at the head.","verifier":"Check the pull request for https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/102 at its head commit. Check out the PR head in your worktree. Find the PR number (for example with `gh api 'repos/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/pulls?state=all&per_page=50' --jq '.[] | [.number, .head.ref, .head.sha, .state] | @tsv'`) and set it as `PR=<number>` in the shell. Run `npm ci` once at the repository root before the checks. Run every command from the repository root. There is exactly one check per expect line, in order. Judge each check only on its stated PASS and FAIL conditions.\n\nCheck 1. Expect: \"packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> prints <file>:<line>: <error> for every row that is not valid JSON (1-based lines, blank lines skipped, an unterminated last line checked) and exits 1 if any, 0 if none\"\nRun:\n`d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"a\":1}\\n\\n{bad\\n[1,2]\\n\\n{\"b\":2' > \"$d/mixed.jsonl\"; node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js \"$d/mixed.jsonl\"; echo \"exit=$?\"`\n`printf '{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\":' > \"$d/torn.jsonl\"; node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js \"$d/torn.jsonl\"; echo \"exit=$?\"`\n`printf '{\"a\":1}\\n\\n[2]\\n\"s\"\\n' > \"$d/clean.jsonl\"; node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js \"$d/clean.jsonl\"; echo \"exit=$?\"`\n`printf '{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\":2}' > \"$d/clean2.jsonl\"; node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js \"$d/clean2.jsonl\"; echo \"exit=$?\"`\nPASS if all of these hold. mixed.jsonl prints exactly two stdout lines, one starting with `<path>:3: ` and one with `<path>:6: `, where `<path>` is the path exactly as passed, and each line has non-empty error text after the prefix; it reports no other line (in particular not the blank lines 2 and 5) and exits 1. torn.jsonl prints exactly one line starting with `<path>:2: ` followed by non-empty error text, and exits 1. clean.jsonl and clean2.jsonl print nothing to stdout and exit 0. FAIL if any of these differ: a missing or extra reported line, a wrong line number (0-based, or blank lines not counted), a blank line reported, the unterminated last row not reported, a different prefix format, or a wrong exit code.\n\nCheck 2. Expect: \"A missing file or no argument prints to stderr and exits 2\"\nRun:\n`node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js > /tmp/jl-out1 2> /tmp/jl-err1; echo \"exit=$?\"; wc -c < /tmp/jl-err1`\n`node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js /nonexistent/nope.jsonl > /tmp/jl-out2 2> /tmp/jl-err2; echo \"exit=$?\"; wc -c < /tmp/jl-err2`\nPASS if both commands print `exit=2` and both stderr files are non-empty (the byte count is greater than 0). FAIL if either exit code is not 2 or either stderr file is empty.\n\nCheck 3. Expect: \"packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js exports lint(text) returning [{ line, error }]\"\nRun:\n`node --input-type=module -e \"import { lint } from './packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js'; const r = lint('{\\\"a\\\":1}\\n\\n{bad\\n[1]\\n{\\\"b\\\":'); console.log(JSON.stringify(r)); console.log(JSON.stringify(lint('{\\\"a\\\":1}\\n\\n[2]\\n')));\"; echo \"exit=$?\"`\nPASS if the import works, the first printed value is an array of exactly two objects, each with a numeric `line` and a non-empty string `error`, the lines are 3 and 5, the second printed value is `[]`, and the command prints `exit=0`. FAIL if `lint` is not a named export, the import fails, the return value is not an array of `{ line, error }` objects, or the line numbers or the empty result differ.\n\nCheck 4. Expect: \"The workspace has its own package.json (jsonl-lint, type module, test script node --test), no runtime dependencies, and node:test tests for a clean file, a bad middle row, a torn last row, blank lines and the exit codes\"\nRun:\n`node -e \"const p=require('./packages/jsonl-lint/package.json'); console.log(p.name, p.type, JSON.stringify(p.scripts&&p.scripts.test), JSON.stringify(p.dependencies||{}))\"`\n`npm test -w jsonl-lint; echo \"exit=$?\"`\nThen read the test files under `packages/jsonl-lint/` (`ls -R packages/jsonl-lint`).\nPASS if all of these hold. The first command prints name `jsonl-lint`, type `module`, a test script that is `node --test` (optionally followed by arguments), and `{}` for dependencies. The workspace tests run under `node:test` and print `exit=0`. The test files import from `node:test` and contain tests that actually exercise each of: a clean file, a bad middle row, a torn (unterminated) last row, blank lines, and the CLI exit codes. FAIL if `package.json` is missing or any field differs, there are runtime dependencies, the tests fail or do not run, or any of the five scenarios has no test.\n\nCheck 5. Expect: \"packages/jsonl-lint/README.md exists and the root README lists the tool\"\nRun: `test -s packages/jsonl-lint/README.md; echo \"exists=$?\"; grep -n 'jsonl-lint' README.md; echo \"grep=$?\"`\nPASS if the output shows `exists=0` and `grep=0`, and the matching root README line presents jsonl-lint as a tool in the repo. FAIL if the workspace README is missing or empty, or the root README does not mention jsonl-lint.\n\nCheck 6. Expect: \"The change is one PR on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test whose body says Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/102, and npm test and the test workflow are green at its head\"\nRun:\n`gh api \"repos/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/pulls/$PR\" --jq '.body, .head.sha, .state'`\n`gh api \"repos/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/commits/$(gh api \"repos/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/pulls/$PR\" --jq .head.sha)/check-runs\" --jq '.check_runs[] | [.name, .status, .conclusion] | @tsv'`\n`git rev-parse HEAD; npm ci && npm test; echo \"exit=$?\"`\nPASS if all of these hold. The change is in a single PR on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test. Its body contains `Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/102`. The `test` check run for the head SHA is `completed` with conclusion `success`. Your checked-out `HEAD` equals that head SHA. `npm ci && npm test` prints `exit=0`, and its output shows the jsonl-lint tests ran rather than `no workspaces under packages/`. FAIL if the change is spread over several PRs or is in no PR, the Closes line is missing or names a different URL, the test workflow is missing, still pending or failed at the head, or `npm test` fails at the head.\n\nAdvisory (does not affect PASS or FAIL):\n- Check whether whitespace-only lines and CRLF line endings are treated as blank and valid respectively. Note what you find, but do not fail on it.\n- Check that `package-lock.json` was updated for the new workspace, since `npm ci` depends on it.\n- Check that the CLI sets `process.exitCode` rather than calling `process.exit()` before stdout is flushed (piping the output of a large bad file through `wc -l` is a quick test)."}